From ece434040f72e1efccfb043d4f9459a6a4401a3c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 17:13:51 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 15/26] arm64: dts: rk3566-pinetab2: wire WiFi to the LuneOS
 bes2600 driver

This tree comments out the vendor bes2600 branch and merges the
cw1200-based BES2600 bring-up instead. LuneOS ships the out-of-tree
bes2600 driver (the bes2600-module recipe), which is further along, so
point the node at that driver instead:

- compatible becomes "bestechnic,bes2600-sdio", which is what the driver
  looks for. It locates the node with a global of_find_compatible_node()
  rather than through the SDIO device's of_node, and binds to the SDIO
  function by device ID, so "reg" is not what matches it.
- device-wakeup-gpios is renamed to the wakeup-gpios the driver actually
  reads (ACTIVE_LOW, as the 6.6 DT had it), and host-wakeup-gpios is
  uncommented. All four GPIO lookups are optional in the driver and fail
  soft, so a name it does not recognise is silently dropped rather than
  refused.

wl_reg and wl_pwrseq (mmc-pwrseq-bes) are deliberately left as they are:
that sequence drives pwrkey, pwren and reset, which is more than the
mmc-pwrseq-simple node LuneOS used on 6.6 did.

CONFIG_CW1200 must stay unset or its SDIO driver, which claims the same
0x2002 device, can bind first.

Deliberately NOT exposing powerup-gpios (regon, gpio0 RK_PC0) to the driver.

bes2600_platform_data_init() requests every GPIO with GPIOD_OUT_LOW:

    pdata->powerup = devm_fwnode_gpiod_get_index(dev, &np->fwnode,
                                                 "powerup", 0, GPIOD_OUT_LOW, ...);

so handing it regon makes the driver drive the chip's power-enable LOW
immediately after mmc-pwrseq has just powered the chip up. The card still
enumerates (pwrseq runs first), then the chip dies partway through the
firmware download and stops answering on SDIO:

    wait dpd data ready failed:-110
    read dpd data failed.
    bes2600_sdio_probe failed, func:1
    probe with driver bes2600_wlan failed with error -123

The LuneOS 6.6 device tree, where WiFi worked, never gave regon to the
driver either - megi's node names it regon-gpios, which the driver does not
look for, so it stayed NULL there too. Same for reset-gpios, which is owned
by the pwrseq; the driver logs "can't request reset_gpio (-2)" and carries
on, exactly as it did on 6.6.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [depends on an out-of-tree driver]
Also restore local-mac-address, which the 6.9 bring-up carried (commit
b7b216e) and the 7.2 migration dropped. Without it bes2600_get_base_mac()
falls back to get_random_bytes(), so wlan0 comes up with a different MAC on
every boot:

    bestechnic,bes2600 device node does not have valid local-mac-address
    property, random mac will be used!

Observed across boots: 62:15:0a:9d:64:02, then 3e:29:7f:bd:56:1c - both with
the locally-administered bit set. mac80211 authenticates with that address
while the firmware keeps its own, and every association is dropped by the AP
with reason 2 (PREV_AUTH_NOT_VALID), on both bands, with the interface never
transmitting a single frame.

The wifi-macaddr-persister service does not help here: it snapshots
/sys/class/net/wlan0/address on first boot, which had already captured a
random value (62:15:0a:9d:64:02), and nothing applies that file back to the
interface.
---
 .../dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinetab2-v2.0.dts     | 30 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinetab2-v2.0.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinetab2-v2.0.dts
index f54afebdb982..ad421edeb39c 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinetab2-v2.0.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3566-pinetab2-v2.0.dts
@@ -93,8 +93,20 @@ &sdmmc1 {
 	#address-cells = <1>;
 	#size-cells = <0>;
 
+	/*
+	 * LuneOS ships the out-of-tree bes2600 driver (bes2600-module) rather than
+	 * the cw1200-based bring-up this tree merges, so this node carries the
+	 * compatible and GPIO names that driver looks for. It finds this node with
+	 * a global of_find_compatible_node() on "bestechnic,bes2600-sdio" and reads
+	 * the reset/powerup/wakeup/host-wakeup GPIOs from it; the SDIO function
+	 * itself is matched by device ID, so "reg" is not what binds the driver.
+	 *
+	 * megi's wl_pwrseq (mmc-pwrseq-bes) is deliberately kept: it drives pwrkey,
+	 * pwren and reset, which is more than the mmc-pwrseq-simple node LuneOS used
+	 * on 6.6 managed.
+	 */
 	bes2600: wifi@1 {
-		compatible = "bestechnic,bes2600";
+		compatible = "bestechnic,bes2600-sdio";
 		reg = <1>;
 		pinctrl-names = "default";
 		pinctrl-0 = <&host_wake_wl &wifi_wake_host_h &wifi_regon>;
@@ -102,9 +114,19 @@ bes2600: wifi@1 {
 		interrupt-parent = <&gpio0>;
 		interrupts = <RK_PC4 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>;
 
-		device-wakeup-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PB7 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>; // host_wake_wl
-		//host-wakeup-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PC4 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>; // wifi_wake_host_h
-		regon-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PC0 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
+		/*
+		 * Without this bes2600_get_base_mac() logs "does not have valid
+		 * local-mac-address property, random mac will be used!" and calls
+		 * get_random_bytes(), so the adapter comes up with a different MAC
+		 * on every boot. mac80211 then authenticates with that address
+		 * while the firmware keeps its own, and the AP drops the
+		 * association with reason 2 (PREV_AUTH_NOT_VALID). This value was
+		 * carried in the 6.9 bring-up and lost in the 7.2 migration.
+		 */
+		local-mac-address = [de ad be ef 12 34];
+		wakeup-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PB7 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>; // host_wake_wl
+		host-wakeup-gpios = <&gpio0 RK_PC4 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>; // wifi_wake_host_h
+		// NO powerup-gpios: see the commit message.
 	};
 };
 
